01:16.080 --> 01:24.520
So today we're going to start the first lecture of two lectures on the topic of motion planning.

01:24.520 --> 01:32.040
As I referred to and mentioned many times, motion planning can be seen as an instantiation

01:32.040 --> 01:38.420
of optimal control problem whereby we account for state constraints, complicated state constraints.

01:38.420 --> 01:42.940
In particular state constraints are in general non-convex constraints.

01:42.940 --> 01:48.400
Motion planning problem at a high level can be defined as a problem of computing a sequence

01:48.400 --> 01:57.000
of actions that drive a robot from an initial condition to a terminal condition while avoiding

01:57.000 --> 02:01.880
obstacles, respecting motion constraints, and possibly optimizing a cost function.

02:01.880 --> 02:08.280
We have already seen techniques that aim at planning motions to respect motion constraints,

02:08.280 --> 02:10.200
possibly optimizing a cost function.

02:10.200 --> 02:16.160
But so far we have not really dealt with the topic of how to handle state constraints.

02:16.160 --> 02:20.560
In particular, as I said, obstacle avoidance constraints that clearly for robotic systems

02:20.560 --> 02:22.940
are of pivotal importance.

02:22.940 --> 02:26.460
So today and on Thursday, we're going to do exactly that.

02:26.460 --> 02:31.480
In particular, the goal of today is to give an introduction to the topic of motion planning,

02:31.480 --> 02:37.400
also with a little bit of historical context, and then to learn about the most basic techniques

02:37.400 --> 02:44.720
in motion planning that are rooted in the notion of discretization and graph search.

02:44.720 --> 02:49.920
These are the very first algorithms that were introduced and are widely used in practice.

02:49.920 --> 02:56.680
For example, Mars rovers use a variant of graph search algorithms to plan the motion

02:56.680 --> 03:02.120
on the surface of Mars, a variant of D star algorithm that we will see later on.

03:02.120 --> 03:05.280
Readings for today.

