One of the main references for motion planning is a book by Steve LaValle, a little bit dated

03:09.800 --> 03:10.800
by now.

03:10.800 --> 03:15.200
It's from 2006, but there has been a lot of progress in the past 10 years.

03:15.200 --> 03:16.400
But still a very good reference.

03:16.400 --> 03:21.320
In particular, the material covered today is taken from chapter 6.

03:21.320 --> 03:25.240
Another very good reference is a book from Dimitri Bersekas on dynamic programming and

03:25.240 --> 03:26.240
optimal control.

03:26.240 --> 03:31.440
In particular, this book has a chapter, chapter 2, that does a very good job at covering search

03:31.440 --> 03:32.680
algorithms.

03:32.680 --> 03:38.500
In particular, the class of search algorithms is referred to as label correcting methods.

03:38.500 --> 03:44.200
It is the class that we're going to cover today.

03:44.200 --> 03:46.560
Motion planning obviously is a big deal in robotics.

03:46.560 --> 03:53.220
Just to give you some context and some excitement, here I'm showing one of my students testing

03:53.220 --> 03:59.100
his motion planning algorithms against a quadrotor.

03:59.100 --> 04:00.500
Suspense with the quadrotor.

04:00.500 --> 04:05.860
The quadrotor is trying to plan its motion, obviously by avoiding a state constraint presented

04:05.860 --> 04:07.820
by this word.

04:07.820 --> 04:12.300
So that's what I really mean by motion planning, trying to reason about motion constraints

04:12.300 --> 04:18.140
and at the same time constraints in the environment that can be even time-varying, adversarial

04:18.140 --> 04:21.420
in this case, uncertain and so on.

04:21.420 --> 04:23.840
Motion planning, as I said, is ubiquitous.

04:23.840 --> 04:28.780
For the sake of this class, our key application domain will be the application of trying to

04:28.780 --> 04:36.020
find a path for the wheeled vehicle that avoids obstacles, but can be used in many other settings.

04:36.220 --> 04:41.540
For example, in order to control a humanoid robot that is trying to grasp a cup, but it

04:41.540 --> 04:45.340
has to account for the fact that the cup might be in a drawer.

04:45.340 --> 04:49.780
So it has to be very carefully not hitting the walls of the drawer.

04:49.780 --> 04:56.060
Another exciting application of motion planning is how to carefully maneuver robotic probes

04:56.060 --> 04:59.020
in order to perform surgeries.

04:59.020 --> 05:03.260
For example, how to maneuver the probe in order to get into the blood vessels and then

05:03.300 --> 05:07.580
deliver a medicine at a very specific target location.

05:07.580 --> 05:11.820
Or it can even be applied to domains that have very little to do with robotics, like

05:11.820 --> 05:14.260
for example, protein folding.

05:14.260 --> 05:20.780
You can think of the problem of protein folding as a problem of sequencing a number of maneuvers

05:20.780 --> 05:23.060
in order to get to the desired protein.

05:23.060 --> 05:26.260
That can be cast, believe it or not, as a motion planning problem.

05:26.260 --> 05:29.660
It's a very general problem, very important.

05:29.660 --> 05:34.780
Not surprisingly, people have been working on it for the past several decades.

05:34.780 --> 05:39.540
The problem was first formulated in the 70s.

05:39.540 --> 05:45.420
And the first exact algorithms, we'll discuss a bit better what I mean by exact algorithms

05:45.420 --> 05:49.980
later on, were developed in the 80s.

05:49.980 --> 05:55.380
The problem with the exact algorithms is that in general they do not scale well to high

05:55.380 --> 05:56.380
dimensional spaces.

05:56.420 --> 06:04.660
By high dimensional, I mean to robots, whereby the configuration factor has a number of dimensions

06:04.660 --> 06:08.100
that is, say, larger than four or five.

06:08.100 --> 06:13.540
So as long as you have a unicycle, you have x, y, theta, three dimensional, it's fine.

06:13.540 --> 06:16.340
But you have kinematics, so it's a little bit more complicated.

06:16.340 --> 06:17.340
Four is fine.

06:17.340 --> 06:19.180
Five tend to be already complicated.

06:19.180 --> 06:25.300
Unfortunately, this problem has inevitably an exponential scaling with respect to the

06:25.420 --> 06:28.660
configuration factor in terms of complexity.

06:28.660 --> 06:33.020
This prompted people in the 90s to look at approximation algorithms.

06:33.020 --> 06:39.780
So algorithms that deliver a solution that is good enough, but the advantage is that

06:39.780 --> 06:41.660
that can be done very quickly.

06:41.660 --> 06:47.700
And this is a recurrent theme in robotics, since real time implementation is of such

06:47.700 --> 06:51.660
an importance, oftentimes they're willing to sacrifice a little bit of the correctness

06:51.700 --> 06:56.380
of the algorithm, or the quality of the output, in favor of real time computation.

06:58.380 --> 07:03.860
2000s have seen widespread application of these methodologies on real systems.

07:03.860 --> 07:11.300
I already mentioned mass rovers, but obviously self-driving cars, drones, you name it.

07:11.300 --> 07:17.140
Basically every robot that moves around has to reason about environment, meaning that

07:17.180 --> 07:22.700
the environment cannot be completely predicted at the design stage, has to run in the form

07:22.700 --> 07:26.900
of another motion planning algorithm.

07:26.900 --> 07:32.020
If you already know exactly what the environment that the robot is going to operate in, then

07:32.020 --> 07:36.460
in principle you can pre-script the trajectory of every configuration, and you don't have

07:36.460 --> 07:38.420
to run a motion planning algorithm.

07:38.420 --> 07:40.980
But in general, that is not possible, so you have to do it.

07:40.980 --> 07:44.900
And in particular, each one of you, every day, runs a motion planning algorithm in your

07:44.900 --> 07:45.900
brain.

07:46.380 --> 07:49.700
This is a very active field of research.

07:49.700 --> 07:54.340
Here is a very partial list of the topics people are currently working on.

07:54.340 --> 07:59.860
For example, inclusion of increasingly sophisticated motion constraints in a motion planning process,

07:59.860 --> 08:01.580
even logical constraints.

08:01.580 --> 08:07.420
What I mean by logical constraints is that as you plan the trajectory for your robot,

08:07.420 --> 08:12.820
you account for logical constraints, like for example, rules of the road.

08:12.820 --> 08:18.260
Think of a self-driving car, that as it plans its maneuver, not only has to obey collision

08:18.260 --> 08:23.980
constraints, but also has to obey rules of the road, cannot move laterally if there is

08:23.980 --> 08:26.580
a solid lane.

08:26.580 --> 08:31.340
Planning under uncertainty, in many applications you have imperfect state information, so you

08:31.340 --> 08:37.700
don't measure directly for the case of unicycle what x, y, and theta are, but you have a noisy

08:37.700 --> 08:39.580
version of x, y, and theta.

08:39.580 --> 08:42.900
So how do you plan your trajectory if, for example, you don't know exactly what your

08:42.900 --> 08:43.900
configuration is?

08:43.900 --> 08:47.100
For example, you don't know exactly what your position is.

08:47.100 --> 08:52.620
Parallel implementation, of course, there has been a lot of development from the hardware

08:52.620 --> 08:56.680
side, for example, with the advent of GPUs.

08:56.680 --> 09:02.700
So one active research topic is how do you design algorithms that are specifically targeting

09:02.700 --> 09:08.180
the platform over which these algorithms will be from?

09:08.180 --> 09:15.340
Which means thinking about parallel versions of the algorithms that can be actively implemented,

09:15.340 --> 09:17.060
for example, in GPUs.

09:17.060 --> 09:22.540
And finally, motion planning, as in general trajectory optimization, is going to deliver

09:22.540 --> 09:25.100
you an open-loop trajectory.

09:25.100 --> 09:31.220
So the question is how do you endow that open-loop trajectory with a feedback tracking, a feedback

09:31.220 --> 09:36.340
correction term, very similar to what we already did in the context of trajectory optimization

09:36.340 --> 09:39.180
through the lens of differential flatness.

09:39.180 --> 09:46.100
Okay, so let's dig a little bit deeper into the problem of motion planning in the simplest

09:46.100 --> 09:47.600
possible setup.

09:47.600 --> 09:53.340
So assume that we have a two-dimensional environment, and then we have a Lego block that we have

09:53.340 --> 09:57.580
to move from an initial condition to a final condition.

09:57.580 --> 10:04.860
So the motion planning problem entails finding a sequence of maneuvers, like, for example,

10:04.860 --> 10:10.140
rotations and rotations that deliver your Lego block at the final destination.

10:10.140 --> 10:17.020
This is a simple problem because it's in 2D, and because you don't have complicated motion

10:17.020 --> 10:18.020
constraints.

10:18.020 --> 10:25.500
You can move the Lego block laterally, you can rotate it as you want, and so on.

10:25.500 --> 10:31.420
Now there are several approaches to motion planning.

10:31.420 --> 10:36.500
The very first one is referred to as potential fields.

10:36.500 --> 10:44.740
The idea is to create potentials whose derivative give forces on the robot, these are vehicle

10:44.740 --> 10:51.380
forces on the robot, that pull it toward the goal and repel it from the obstacles.

10:51.380 --> 11:01.220
So think of creating, let's assume that this is your 2D environment.

11:01.220 --> 11:06.380
So this is an obstacle, this is the goal.

11:06.380 --> 11:16.300
So think about, actually, let me turn the perspective, maybe like this.

11:16.300 --> 11:20.060
So this is your obstacle, this is your goal.

11:20.060 --> 11:26.060
So the idea is to create artificial potentials, so basically hills around the obstacles, and

11:26.060 --> 11:31.700
the valleys around the goal.

11:31.700 --> 11:37.580
So that you can think then of a robot as a sort of a ball that is moving in this kind

11:37.580 --> 11:39.900
of a hill-shaped environment.

11:39.900 --> 11:45.240
It is repelled from the obstacles and is attracted toward the goal.

11:45.240 --> 11:49.940
Now the problem is that if you don't design these hills well, there is a danger that your

11:49.940 --> 11:53.780
ball gets stuck in a local minimap and never reaches the goal.

11:53.820 --> 11:59.580
So that's one of the main difficulties with the potential field motion planning.

11:59.580 --> 12:03.940
The advantage is that it is very intuitive, and there are different ways of setting the

12:03.940 --> 12:08.100
hills, but usually that is very intuitive design, so this is good.

12:08.100 --> 12:13.020
It's extremely fast, the only thing that you have to do in order to control the robot is

12:13.020 --> 12:21.380
at each point in the state space, you look at where you are, you compute the gradient

12:21.380 --> 12:25.460
of your potential, that basically gives you the direction with respect to which your motion

12:25.460 --> 12:30.060
should go, and then you just follow it for a couple of, for a one-time step.

12:30.060 --> 12:33.900
So from a computational standpoint, it is extremely effective.

12:33.900 --> 12:40.380
On the other hand, it's prone to this issue of getting stuck in a local minimap, so designing

12:40.380 --> 12:45.300
potential so that you don't have this issue is extremely tricky.

12:45.300 --> 12:50.100
This has prompted the design of other classes of planning algorithms.

12:50.140 --> 12:56.740
Another approach, and this approach is extremely popular, is to take the problem that is naturally

12:56.740 --> 13:03.980
defined in a continuous space, and then discretize the environment, and turn the problem of motion

13:03.980 --> 13:11.260
planning in the problem of searching on a graph, whereby the graph represents the underlying

13:11.260 --> 13:14.580
discretization of your environment.

13:14.580 --> 13:17.140
And this is something that we're going to look into in more detail.

13:17.140 --> 13:23.300
So this class of algorithms belong to the Jackson algorithm for motion planning, A-star,

13:23.300 --> 13:25.580
D-star, and so on.

13:25.580 --> 13:27.980
Very popular in robotics.

13:27.980 --> 13:32.620
The third class is referred to as combinatorial planning.

13:32.620 --> 13:39.380
So here the idea is to construct structures that allow you to discretize the problem,

13:39.380 --> 13:44.260
yet retain all the information that is needed in order to solve the motion planning problem.

13:44.260 --> 13:47.980
So basically, the idea is to have a discretization that is lossless.

13:47.980 --> 13:52.620
If you're able to solve the discretized problem, the solution that you get will be the same

13:52.620 --> 13:59.820
as if you were solving the continuous problem, different from the grid planning approach,

13:59.820 --> 14:07.300
whereby you have some approximation in the output that you compute.

14:07.300 --> 14:12.260
Now this is great, because basically you're turning a problem into something you can do

14:12.260 --> 14:17.900
computation with, without losing any information that stems from the continuous formulation

14:17.900 --> 14:19.620
of the problem.

14:19.620 --> 14:28.980
The disadvantage is that the computation of the data structures is in general complicated.

14:28.980 --> 14:32.540
It doesn't scale well with the dimension of the problem.

14:32.540 --> 14:37.860
So this is the reason why actually this approach is not very widely used in practice.

14:37.860 --> 14:41.180
It doesn't really scale to most of the problems that are of practical interest.

14:41.180 --> 14:43.780
But in some cases, it's still useful.

14:43.780 --> 14:51.380
And finally, a fourth class of motion planning is represented by simply-based planning, which

14:51.380 --> 14:59.260
tries to somehow merge the advantages of combinatorial planning with grid-based planning.

14:59.260 --> 15:06.580
So the idea is to still try to reason about the continuous problem, but do computation

15:06.580 --> 15:10.180
with respect to a discretization that is a little bit more adaptive than what you would

15:10.180 --> 15:12.140
do with a standard grid-based approach.

15:12.140 --> 15:14.060
And we'll see this in much more detail.

15:14.060 --> 15:20.060
In particular, in this class, we're going to focus on these three classes of planning

15:20.060 --> 15:24.180
algorithms, grid-based, combinatorial, and sampling-based.

15:24.180 --> 15:27.420
In particular today, we're going to talk about grid-based and combinatorial.

15:27.420 --> 15:33.500
And on Thursday, we're going to talk about sampling-based, and then going to summarize

15:33.500 --> 15:38.700
all of them and draw a conclusion about the relative advantages and disadvantages.

15:38.740 --> 15:39.740
So that's a plan.

15:43.460 --> 15:47.940
All right, so let's start with the grid-based approaches.

15:47.940 --> 15:52.300
Grid-based approaches are arguably the most natural one.

15:52.300 --> 15:58.460
So what you do is you have a motion planning problem that

15:58.460 --> 16:06.460
is defined over a continuous space, say for example, a square.

16:06.540 --> 16:14.540
And then you have obstacles here and there, something like that.

16:14.540 --> 16:17.340
So this is your start point.

16:17.340 --> 16:19.740
This is your goal region.

16:19.740 --> 16:23.380
So what you do is just to discretize the problem.

16:23.380 --> 16:25.900
So you just grid your environment.

16:34.700 --> 16:38.660
You put a representative point within each cell.

16:49.500 --> 16:50.380
And so on.

16:50.380 --> 16:54.380
You connect these points, for example,

16:54.380 --> 16:58.580
by using four neighbors or maybe eight neighbors.

17:05.180 --> 17:10.300
And then you clip your goal and your start position

17:10.300 --> 17:12.060
to the closest point on the grid.

17:15.620 --> 17:18.580
So having done that, what you're left with

17:18.580 --> 17:25.540
is a graph, meaning a set of vertices, these points over here,

17:25.540 --> 17:33.300
and a set of edges, the links between these points.

17:33.300 --> 17:35.180
So now the motion planning problem.

17:35.180 --> 17:36.780
Yes?

17:36.780 --> 17:40.780
What do you discretize to?

17:40.780 --> 17:45.100
So that's one of the pain points of grid-based approaches,

17:45.100 --> 17:48.060
is that the quality of the solution in general

17:48.060 --> 17:50.900
depends on the resolution.

17:50.900 --> 17:53.500
You have to be a little bit careful with,

17:53.500 --> 17:55.220
I'll come back to this in a second,

17:55.220 --> 17:57.580
on how you choose your neighbors.

17:57.580 --> 18:00.340
But in general, as the resolution gets smaller

18:00.340 --> 18:03.780
and smaller, then the solution that you compute

18:03.780 --> 18:06.220
is going to converge to the one that you will compute

18:06.220 --> 18:07.980
in a continuous problem.

18:07.980 --> 18:10.940
The question is that as the resolution gets smaller,

18:10.940 --> 18:14.620
then the number of vertices gets larger, which basically means

18:14.620 --> 18:16.460
you run more computation, which basically means

18:16.460 --> 18:20.220
that this becomes more compute intense.

18:20.220 --> 18:21.980
So there is a trade-off.

18:21.980 --> 18:24.780
Nobody tells you what the trade-off is there.

18:24.780 --> 18:27.140
It really also depends on the obstacle space.

18:27.140 --> 18:29.140
So indeed, the resolution is something

18:29.140 --> 18:31.740
that is very difficult to tune.

18:31.740 --> 18:35.180
So what people do is a little bit of trial and error,

18:35.180 --> 18:41.380
even if you have time, and also try to do adaptive grids,

18:41.380 --> 18:44.340
whereby you have a finer discretization

18:44.340 --> 18:46.700
in regions of the environment when you think

18:46.700 --> 18:49.620
it's more promising to do motion planning and coarser

18:49.620 --> 18:50.380
discretization.

18:50.380 --> 18:53.700
So there is a lot of work on dealing with that issue.

18:53.700 --> 18:56.100
But in general, there is not an exact answer.

18:56.100 --> 18:58.700
It is one of the difficulties and disadvantages

18:58.700 --> 19:00.260
of grid-based approaches.

19:00.260 --> 19:01.660
What I was saying is that you have

19:01.660 --> 19:03.460
to be careful with the neighbors,

19:03.460 --> 19:09.300
is that consider this example, whereby we

19:09.300 --> 19:19.260
start on a square, discretize, so you start here

19:19.260 --> 19:20.260
and you want to get here.

19:20.260 --> 19:21.420
So this is the goal.

19:21.420 --> 19:23.220
This is the start.

19:23.220 --> 19:27.140
And so we consider as the points in the vertices

19:27.140 --> 19:29.620
these points here.

19:29.620 --> 19:33.460
And we only connect them by looking at the four neighbors.

19:33.460 --> 19:35.960
So essentially, we consider this connection, this connection,

19:35.960 --> 19:37.020
and this connection.

19:37.020 --> 19:41.260
We don't consider the diagonal connections.

19:41.260 --> 19:44.460
So we want to get from here to here.

19:44.460 --> 19:50.300
And we assume that each edge is of length 1.

19:50.300 --> 19:55.300
So the optimal path, meaning this path,

19:55.300 --> 19:58.700
has length square root of 2.

19:58.700 --> 20:04.300
But the problem is that any path that I consider in this grid

20:04.300 --> 20:08.940
to go to the goal has length what?

20:08.940 --> 20:09.820
What's that?

20:09.820 --> 20:14.740
2, because at least I have to go one up and one right.

20:14.740 --> 20:17.180
And this doesn't really depend on the resolution.

20:17.180 --> 20:29.700
If I make the resolution finer and finer,

20:29.700 --> 20:32.860
so now these will be my connections.

20:32.860 --> 20:34.860
I still have this issue that at the very least I

20:34.860 --> 20:36.980
have to go one up and one right.

20:36.980 --> 20:39.900
So basically, the length of the optimal solution

20:39.900 --> 20:43.460
that I get by optimizing over this graph

20:43.460 --> 20:45.840
is going to be equal to 2, no matter

20:45.840 --> 20:47.740
how small the resolution is.

20:50.700 --> 20:53.020
But you will say, OK, so then the right way to do it

20:53.020 --> 20:57.140
is actually to also include for the diagonal motions, which

20:57.140 --> 20:59.220
is what people typically do.

20:59.220 --> 21:00.860
The point of this example is to tell you

21:00.860 --> 21:06.820
that some care has to be taken in order to come up

21:06.820 --> 21:10.100
with the right neighborhood.

21:10.100 --> 21:11.980
Having said that, then the resolution

21:11.980 --> 21:14.260
is really a trade-off between the quality of the solution

21:14.260 --> 21:15.900
and computation time.

21:15.900 --> 21:19.660
What is the right solution is experience,

21:19.660 --> 21:22.700
and there are some guidelines out there as well.

21:22.700 --> 21:27.460
The key point is that the resolution problem

21:27.460 --> 21:29.700
notwithstanding, we have translated the motion planning

21:30.420 --> 21:34.140
into the problem of searching a path over a grid, so something

21:34.140 --> 21:40.700
that is a typical problem in the field of computer science.

21:40.700 --> 21:42.060
So in other words, we have turned

21:42.060 --> 21:46.220
a problem of motion planning into a graph search problem.

21:46.220 --> 21:47.580
So then the next logical question

21:47.580 --> 21:53.860
is, how do we best search a path over this graph?

21:53.860 --> 21:55.420
So the class of algorithms that we're

21:55.420 --> 21:57.900
going to consider in this class is a very broad class

21:57.940 --> 22:02.420
of algorithms referred to as label-correcting algorithms.

22:02.420 --> 22:10.500
Where the idea is to label each node in the graph

22:10.500 --> 22:14.020
with a function, so this is the node q,

22:14.020 --> 22:20.740
with a function c of q that represents the cost to arrive

22:20.740 --> 22:24.260
to this node from the origin, meaning

22:24.260 --> 22:27.740
from the start point of your motion planning problem.

22:27.740 --> 22:32.100
We refer to the best path that we have found so far.

22:32.100 --> 22:37.180
And the idea is to iteratively improve over these levels

22:37.180 --> 22:39.740
until we find an optimal path to the goal.

22:39.740 --> 22:45.140
In particular, the way it works is relatively simple.

22:45.140 --> 22:48.460
So this is also referred to as a cost to arrive.

22:48.460 --> 23:01.540
As I said, is the cost to arrive to node q

23:01.540 --> 23:04.140
with respect to the best path that we've found so far,

23:04.140 --> 23:05.940
which is not necessarily the optimal one.

23:05.940 --> 23:08.460
This is something that we're going to iteratively improve

23:08.460 --> 23:11.060
along the algorithm, but it's the best one

23:11.060 --> 23:12.620
that we have found so far.

23:12.620 --> 23:17.180
If we haven't found any path to a given node, say q prime,

23:17.180 --> 23:19.540
then the algorithm is simply initialized

23:19.540 --> 23:23.020
with the cq prime equal infinity.

23:23.020 --> 23:26.140
And then progressively, this level goes down.

23:26.140 --> 23:28.060
Of course, in practice, you cannot code infinity

23:28.060 --> 23:29.300
in the computer.

23:29.300 --> 23:33.700
It will be a very large number, so say one trillion.

23:33.700 --> 23:35.820
So the way these algorithms work is

23:35.820 --> 23:42.500
that we maintain a list of nodes or vertices.

23:42.500 --> 23:46.700
I'm going to use the words nodes or vertices equivalently.

23:46.740 --> 23:49.700
Into a queue, depends everything in the community

23:49.700 --> 23:53.060
as either frontier queue, allowed queue, priority queue.

23:53.060 --> 23:54.740
These are all synonyms.

23:54.740 --> 24:02.460
So this is a list of nodes that we are currently considering

24:02.460 --> 24:07.900
as milestones, meaning as nodes that are included

24:07.900 --> 24:10.620
in the optimal path to the goal.

24:10.620 --> 24:13.020
So I currently note that we are considering

24:13.020 --> 24:15.860
for further exploration in order to see whether they

24:15.860 --> 24:19.740
should belong or not to the optimal path to the goal.

24:19.740 --> 24:22.580
And the idea of level-correcting algorithms then is as follows.

24:22.580 --> 24:27.460
Is to iterate on the nodes, and in particular, at each step,

24:27.460 --> 24:31.500
we extract a node q, or vertex q, as I said.

24:31.500 --> 24:33.460
Node and vertices are the same.

24:33.460 --> 24:38.540
We extract the vertex from the frontier queue,

24:38.540 --> 24:41.820
and we look at all its neighbors.

24:41.860 --> 24:43.860
So assume...

24:54.340 --> 25:02.220
So we extract node q, for which we have a label Cq,

25:02.220 --> 25:06.940
and we look at all its neighbors, q prime.

25:06.940 --> 25:12.420
q prime 1, q prime 2, q prime 3, and so on.

25:12.420 --> 25:16.540
These are nodes that can be reached from the origin

25:16.540 --> 25:22.660
by going through q first, and then in one step to q prime.

25:22.660 --> 25:26.220
And then we ask the following question.

25:26.220 --> 25:30.340
Is it possible to get to q prime?

25:30.340 --> 25:36.940
So now we focus on a specific neighbor.

25:43.180 --> 25:44.820
That will be q prime.

25:44.820 --> 25:49.540
So is it possible to get to q prime through q in a way

25:49.540 --> 25:52.780
that is cheaper than the current path that I currently

25:52.780 --> 25:54.580
have found for q prime?

25:54.580 --> 26:01.900
Meaning, is c of q plus the cost of going from q to q prime

26:01.900 --> 26:06.820
directly less than c of q prime?

26:10.580 --> 26:15.660
Again, what this means, don't consider for now upper.

26:15.660 --> 26:17.580
Consider just this.

26:17.580 --> 26:24.220
So what this is saying is, can I get to q prime

26:24.220 --> 26:27.140
by going through q and then directly to q prime

26:27.140 --> 26:30.460
with a cost that is less than the cost of the path

26:30.460 --> 26:34.380
that I have found in some previous iteration?

26:34.380 --> 26:40.340
If yes, let's revise the path to q prime with this new path

26:40.340 --> 26:42.740
that we have just found.

26:42.740 --> 26:43.940
Does it make sense?

26:43.940 --> 26:48.060
That's one of the core routines in the label-correcting

26:48.060 --> 26:50.380
algorithms.

26:50.380 --> 26:53.340
Any questions about this?

26:54.060 --> 26:57.860
So the notation cqq prime is from q to q prime?

26:57.860 --> 26:59.980
In one step.

26:59.980 --> 27:01.820
So in the case of the graph, we'll

27:01.820 --> 27:03.660
be going diagonal one step.

27:09.180 --> 27:11.740
I have a question about the grid formation stuff.

27:11.740 --> 27:15.100
What's the rule of the outer boundary of your robot

27:15.100 --> 27:16.660
when you're forming this grid?

27:16.660 --> 27:18.700
Are you assuming it's just a point moving along?

27:18.700 --> 27:20.300
Yeah, no, very good question.

27:20.340 --> 27:26.340
So typically, once we discretize the problem this way,

27:26.340 --> 27:30.820
we are essentially viewing the robot as a point.

27:30.820 --> 27:34.340
But in general, the robot is not a point.

27:34.340 --> 27:37.020
So for example, a typical thing to do

27:37.020 --> 27:40.780
is that if you're a robot with some weird shape,

27:40.780 --> 27:46.460
say with six wheels, like a Mars rover, then what you do,

27:46.460 --> 27:51.300
you be like a circle, so an over-approximating object.

27:51.300 --> 27:54.300
And then you inflate all obstacles

27:54.300 --> 27:56.620
by the radius of the circle.

27:56.620 --> 27:59.020
And then you shrink the robot to a point.

27:59.020 --> 28:00.900
Does it make sense?

28:00.900 --> 28:04.300
This is basically what is done on the Mars rovers.

28:04.300 --> 28:05.500
I can't tell you the radius.

28:05.500 --> 28:08.980
For whatever reason, that is considered a script.

28:08.980 --> 28:12.980
So but you can assume what the radius could be, right?

28:12.980 --> 28:13.480
Yeah?

28:13.480 --> 28:15.540
In the practice, what is your cost of arrival?

28:15.540 --> 28:17.580
Is that like path length?

28:17.580 --> 28:21.620
Yeah, typically it's path length in the setting of.

28:21.620 --> 28:23.780
So there are versions of grid-based algorithms

28:23.780 --> 28:27.780
that also account for motion constraints.

28:27.780 --> 28:30.340
Here, we're not accounting for any dynamics and so on.

28:30.340 --> 28:32.140
This is a purely geometric setting

28:32.140 --> 28:34.500
in which a typical cost is just the path length.

28:34.500 --> 28:37.540
OK, so then when you're doing this algorithm,

28:37.540 --> 28:40.740
is the robot actually going down that path

28:40.740 --> 28:42.860
and just going back and forth?

28:42.860 --> 28:43.780
Or how is it?

28:43.780 --> 28:44.280
Right.

28:44.320 --> 28:48.480
How can it know ahead of time without traveling there?

28:48.480 --> 28:50.880
So you have found a path like this one.

28:50.880 --> 28:52.400
You've already found that, right?

28:52.400 --> 28:52.920
Oh, yes.

28:52.920 --> 28:57.200
So we're assuming that we have already

28:57.200 --> 28:59.520
found another path to q prime.

28:59.520 --> 29:03.640
If we have not, remember that we initialized all nodes

29:03.640 --> 29:04.720
with infinity?

29:04.720 --> 29:08.480
It means that this is certainly the best-found path

29:08.480 --> 29:10.880
that we've found so far, since there is no other paths.

29:10.880 --> 29:12.800
So we just consider this one.

29:12.800 --> 29:13.960
Does it make sense?

29:14.600 --> 29:17.120
So at the beginning, we initialized the cost to arrive

29:17.120 --> 29:18.840
for all nodes as infinity.

29:18.840 --> 29:22.880
Yeah, so then you just full pick a node and travel there?

29:22.880 --> 29:25.080
No, we don't.

29:25.080 --> 29:28.800
So the way that it works is that you start from,

29:28.800 --> 29:30.680
so this is an iterative algorithm.

29:30.680 --> 29:35.640
So we start from the start, and we put the start node

29:35.640 --> 29:37.400
into the open list.

29:37.400 --> 29:39.560
At the beginning, there is only one.

29:39.560 --> 29:44.320
We pull it out, and we look at all its neighbors.

29:44.320 --> 29:46.640
And then we say, for all its neighbors,

29:46.640 --> 29:50.200
is the cost to arrive to this node,

29:50.200 --> 29:52.360
the current cost to arrive to this node,

29:52.360 --> 29:54.560
less or larger than the cost to arrive to this node

29:54.560 --> 29:57.160
by going to start and then doing this one step?

29:57.160 --> 30:00.080
The answer is yes, because we're internationalizing all the costs

30:00.080 --> 30:01.320
to arrive with infinity.

30:01.320 --> 30:04.520
So we start building a tentative path, this path.

30:04.520 --> 30:06.800
And then we put this node into the open list.

30:06.800 --> 30:09.360
We do the same for this one and the same for this one.

30:09.360 --> 30:11.280
So at the end of the first iteration,

30:11.280 --> 30:13.440
we have these three nodes in the open list.

30:13.440 --> 30:17.400
And then we start extracting from the open list this one,

30:17.400 --> 30:19.360
and then we look at its neighbors.

30:19.360 --> 30:24.400
Look at it now and considering for the second time this node.

30:24.400 --> 30:27.080
My last question is, how can you know the cost of arrival

30:27.080 --> 30:30.440
to the neighbors without going in there?

30:30.440 --> 30:33.000
How will people know what the cost of arrival

30:33.000 --> 30:35.960
is when it's sitting at that node?

30:35.960 --> 30:41.880
So this is the start position for your problem.

30:41.880 --> 30:44.440
And this is the goal position for your problem.

30:44.440 --> 30:46.200
And you have a map on environment,

30:46.200 --> 30:47.840
so you have a notion of learning.

30:47.840 --> 30:49.360
Oh, you have a map.

30:49.360 --> 30:49.840
Oh, yes.

30:49.840 --> 30:51.240
Oh, it's a known environment.

30:51.240 --> 30:51.720
Oh, yeah.

30:51.720 --> 30:54.120
So maybe this was not here.

30:54.120 --> 30:55.520
So thank you for the question.

30:55.520 --> 30:57.320
Yeah, absolutely.

30:57.320 --> 30:58.920
I thought it was an unknown environment.

30:58.920 --> 30:59.800
No, no, no.

30:59.800 --> 31:01.000
That's a very good point.

31:01.000 --> 31:03.080
And thank you very much for that question.

31:03.080 --> 31:05.920
So now let's take a question, which is a very good one.

31:05.920 --> 31:10.000
So here, so this is your start position.

31:10.000 --> 31:12.600
This is your goal position.

31:12.600 --> 31:18.480
So from here, it seems that you don't have the visibility

31:18.480 --> 31:20.000
for what you have here.

31:20.000 --> 31:23.640
So how do you know if you can travel this corridor or not?

31:23.640 --> 31:25.800
So in the standard motion planning formulation,

31:25.800 --> 31:28.280
we're assuming that we have a static formulation.

31:28.280 --> 31:29.840
So the map of the environment is known.

31:29.840 --> 31:32.760
So the input to the algorithm is the full specification

31:32.760 --> 31:34.000
of the problem.

31:34.000 --> 31:38.160
So the full specification of obstacles and so on.

31:38.160 --> 31:39.640
That's a standard setting.

31:39.640 --> 31:46.080
Now, which, for example, in some applications

31:46.080 --> 31:49.920
like warehousing robotics and so on is a very good one.

31:49.920 --> 31:53.080
But say that you have a robot that is exploring an environment.

31:53.080 --> 31:56.480
Obviously, that robot does not have information

31:56.480 --> 32:02.320
beyond its visibility core, which means that now you're

32:02.320 --> 32:04.120
planning under occlusions.

32:04.120 --> 32:05.280
That's the term.

32:05.280 --> 32:08.160
Which is very much still a state-of-the-art topic.

32:08.160 --> 32:10.800
So in that case, what you do is you plan a trajectory

32:10.800 --> 32:13.800
within the known or observed space.

32:13.800 --> 32:16.680
So this will be the environment with respect

32:16.680 --> 32:18.440
to which you do motion planning.

32:18.440 --> 32:23.240
And then you are careful in putting some intermediate goals

32:23.240 --> 32:25.200
at the frontier of the environment.

32:25.200 --> 32:28.360
The frontier of the environment is what divides what is seen

32:28.360 --> 32:29.400
from what is unknown.

32:29.400 --> 32:31.120
And there are different ways of doing it.

32:31.120 --> 32:33.560
And that gives rise to different algorithms.

32:33.560 --> 32:34.880
This is a very different problem.

32:34.880 --> 32:37.400
For example, when a self-driving car approaches

32:37.400 --> 32:40.720
an intersection, part of the scene might be occluded.

32:40.720 --> 32:49.080
For example, so this is an intersection.

32:49.080 --> 32:50.400
So this is your car.

32:50.400 --> 32:54.280
Your car is not seeing what is here.

32:54.280 --> 32:57.000
So that's what it means to be planning under occlusions.

32:57.000 --> 32:59.320
So this is beyond the scope of this class.

32:59.320 --> 33:02.600
But this is, in many ways, still a research problem

33:02.600 --> 33:05.840
and a very valuable problem.

33:05.840 --> 33:08.440
Is there anything else here?

33:08.440 --> 33:09.920
All right, cool.

33:09.920 --> 33:11.080
Yeah, so please ask questions.

33:11.080 --> 33:14.160
Because sometimes I take things for granted,

33:14.160 --> 33:16.960
having done this stuff for several years.

33:16.960 --> 33:19.920
But I acknowledge that sometimes things cannot solve this.

33:19.920 --> 33:23.360
All right, so we take a note.

33:23.360 --> 33:25.480
We said there's not Q prime.

33:25.480 --> 33:28.600
We may have a path that we have already completed

33:28.640 --> 33:29.480
to this note.

33:29.480 --> 33:33.800
In this case, we refer to the cost of the path as CQ prime.

33:33.800 --> 33:36.560
If you don't have that, CQ prime is set to infinity.

33:36.560 --> 33:38.080
And then we ask the question,

33:38.080 --> 33:42.000
is the cost of the path Q prime through Q lower

33:42.000 --> 33:44.400
than the cost of the path we currently have?

33:44.400 --> 33:46.160
If you don't have it, there will be infinity.

33:46.160 --> 33:51.160
If yes, then we update the level of node Q prime

33:52.320 --> 33:57.560
with the cost of the path that we have just found.

33:57.560 --> 34:02.000
And then we put Q prime into the open list

34:02.000 --> 34:04.960
unless Q prime is the gold node,

34:04.960 --> 34:07.360
in which case we have found the path to the gold.

34:09.040 --> 34:09.880
And we just iterate.

34:09.880 --> 34:12.520
So we start with the start node,

34:12.520 --> 34:14.640
we expand to its neighbors,

34:14.640 --> 34:17.560
and then we keep essentially moving upwards.

34:17.560 --> 34:19.160
So it's a sort of a wave front.

34:21.440 --> 34:22.920
So I would claim it's a very intuitive

34:22.920 --> 34:24.880
and reasonable algorithm.

34:24.880 --> 34:26.000
Any other questions?

34:28.560 --> 34:32.440
So why I refer to the level correcting algorithms

34:32.440 --> 34:35.440
as a class of algorithms instead of a single algorithm?

34:35.440 --> 34:38.640
Because indeed, there are a number of degrees of freedom

34:38.640 --> 34:42.200
in how you select the node

34:42.200 --> 34:44.120
that you extract from the open list.

34:45.080 --> 34:46.960
In general, you may have many.

34:46.960 --> 34:48.440
Which one do you pick?

34:48.440 --> 34:52.120
Randomly, with some more clever strategy, and some.

34:52.120 --> 34:55.000
And how you strengthen some steps.

34:55.200 --> 35:00.200
For example, here, upper is an additional variable

35:00.240 --> 35:04.440
that encodes a notion of the cost of the best path

35:04.440 --> 35:06.600
that we've found so far to the goal.

35:08.000 --> 35:11.640
Initially, upper is set to equal to infinity.

35:11.640 --> 35:16.520
But then as we progress, we may find a path to the goal.

35:16.520 --> 35:18.840
We don't know yet if that is the optimal path.

35:18.840 --> 35:20.800
But say that we find a path to the goal.

35:21.800 --> 35:25.840
So then the idea is that there is no reason

35:25.840 --> 35:28.360
to further considering a node Q prime

35:28.360 --> 35:31.640
if you already know that the cost to arrive to Q prime

35:31.640 --> 35:36.080
is larger than the cost of a path to arrive to the goal

35:36.080 --> 35:37.200
that we have already found.

35:37.200 --> 35:39.680
Simply because we're assuming that the cost is not negative.

35:39.680 --> 35:43.360
So if the cost to arrive to Q prime is already larger

35:43.360 --> 35:46.240
than the cost of a path to the goal that we already found,

35:46.240 --> 35:48.600
there is no hope that an optimal path

35:48.600 --> 35:50.360
is going to go to that node Q prime.

35:51.720 --> 35:54.520
Make sense?

35:54.520 --> 35:56.680
An equation or you want a drawing?

35:59.320 --> 36:03.720
Upper is basically an upper bound on the optimal cost.

36:03.720 --> 36:05.480
Initially set to infinity.

36:05.480 --> 36:08.680
As soon as we find the path to the goal,

36:08.680 --> 36:10.720
we set it to the cost of that path.

36:13.480 --> 36:16.640
And then we discard the nodes whose cost to arrive

36:16.640 --> 36:19.120
is greater than an upper bound on cost.

36:19.160 --> 36:21.600
Because there is no point in considering those paths

36:21.600 --> 36:23.960
because it's guaranteed that an optimal path

36:23.960 --> 36:27.480
is not going to go through that point or node of vertex.

36:29.880 --> 36:31.520
All right.

36:31.520 --> 36:32.680
So this is how it works.

36:35.160 --> 36:37.840
This is the start position, this is the goal.

36:37.840 --> 36:41.040
The blue nodes are the ones that are currently

36:41.040 --> 36:44.160
in the open list and are considered for further evaluation.

36:44.160 --> 36:48.600
And you see how this algorithm basically expand upward.

36:49.480 --> 36:53.120
Keeping evaluating new neighbors at each stage.

36:54.800 --> 36:58.960
The color basically denotes the cost.

36:58.960 --> 37:03.960
Red means highest, green means lower.

37:04.680 --> 37:07.000
Meaning that we are very close to the goal.

37:07.000 --> 37:09.400
Does it require that you complete the entire path

37:09.400 --> 37:11.600
before you start a movement,

37:11.600 --> 37:14.920
or you can do this as you're moving through the world?

37:14.920 --> 37:17.720
Oh yeah, you can do it.

37:18.720 --> 37:22.520
But the danger is that you may not find

37:22.520 --> 37:24.680
that the a priori optimal path.

37:24.680 --> 37:25.680
But certainly.

37:26.760 --> 37:29.880
And as I said, most likely instead of doing that,

37:29.880 --> 37:34.320
what you would do is to have a final discretization here,

37:34.320 --> 37:36.920
and then a coarser discretization here,

37:36.920 --> 37:39.960
and then move a little bit and then recompute.

37:39.960 --> 37:42.040
With the idea that what matters is that

37:42.040 --> 37:44.440
you're very careful about what is around you.

37:44.440 --> 37:45.880
So yes, there are a lot of variations,

37:45.880 --> 37:48.280
but here in the philosophy of this class

37:48.280 --> 37:49.600
I want to give you the main concepts.

37:49.600 --> 37:50.440
Yeah.

37:51.680 --> 37:55.840
When do you know when to stop picking up notes?

37:55.840 --> 38:00.840
Right, so let me first summarize the algorithm,

38:02.080 --> 38:04.280
and then I'll come back to that question.

38:04.280 --> 38:07.520
So this is just a summary of what I've just discussed so far

38:07.520 --> 38:10.200
is mostly for such consistency of the slides.

38:10.200 --> 38:11.880
The way the algorithm works is that

38:11.880 --> 38:14.840
we remove a note from the frontier queue,

38:14.840 --> 38:16.520
and for each child or neighbor,

38:16.520 --> 38:18.520
child or neighbor are synonyms,

38:18.520 --> 38:20.000
we execute the step two,

38:20.000 --> 38:22.240
which is whether we see if we've found

38:22.240 --> 38:26.080
a better path to Q prime than what we had before.

38:28.160 --> 38:32.720
At some point, the frontier is going to become depleted

38:32.720 --> 38:36.960
of notes, so it's empty.

38:36.960 --> 38:39.120
At that point we terminate the algorithm.

38:39.120 --> 38:40.440
So then the natural equation is

38:40.440 --> 38:43.800
once the frontier queue is empty,

38:43.840 --> 38:45.760
and we have terminated the algorithm,

38:47.200 --> 38:48.520
are we guaranteed to have a path?

38:48.520 --> 38:50.920
In particular, are we guaranteed to have the optimal path

38:50.920 --> 38:52.200
with respect to the vertex,

38:52.200 --> 38:54.640
with respect to the discretization?

38:55.560 --> 38:58.320
And the answer is, oops, here.

38:58.320 --> 38:59.320
The answer is yes.

39:00.200 --> 39:02.560
Actually you can prove, we're not going to do a proof,

39:02.560 --> 39:07.040
that once, if a feasible path exists

39:07.040 --> 39:09.640
between the start and goal notes,

39:09.640 --> 39:12.560
here the start note is referred to as QI,

39:12.560 --> 39:15.920
Q init, Q initial, and Q goal,

39:15.920 --> 39:18.160
then the algorithm terminates in finite time,

39:18.160 --> 39:21.960
so it does not start oscillating or doing weird stuff,

39:21.960 --> 39:24.800
and the cost of the path that you find to the goal

39:24.800 --> 39:26.760
is indeed the optimal cost to the goal.

39:27.800 --> 39:29.840
That's a correctness that applies

39:29.840 --> 39:33.840
to all variations of the level-correcting algorithms.

39:33.840 --> 39:35.320
But there are several variations,

39:35.320 --> 39:38.720
depending on how you pull nodes out of the queue.

39:38.720 --> 39:39.560
You had a question?

39:39.600 --> 39:42.480
I'm trying to find them, but it seems pretty terrible.

39:42.480 --> 39:44.560
They guarantee you.

39:44.560 --> 39:46.160
Well, no, I mean, because some algorithms

39:46.160 --> 39:48.360
could be actually guaranteed to converge

39:48.360 --> 39:50.480
only in infinite time, only syntactically.

39:50.480 --> 39:53.160
Yeah, we see that, for example, sampling-based algorithms

39:53.160 --> 39:56.920
are guaranteed to find a solution that is feasible

39:56.920 --> 39:59.960
or that is optimal only when the number of steps

39:59.960 --> 40:00.800
goes to infinity.

40:01.960 --> 40:05.120
No, actually these algorithms are incredibly fast,

40:05.120 --> 40:05.960
super fast.

40:06.960 --> 40:09.400
So, running the algorithm per se

40:09.400 --> 40:11.200
is not what consumes time.

40:11.200 --> 40:13.400
What consumes time is to build the discretization

40:13.400 --> 40:14.760
and represent the obstacles.

40:15.960 --> 40:18.000
But let me talk a little bit about the differences

40:18.000 --> 40:21.720
about the implementation of level-correcting algorithms.

40:23.080 --> 40:24.880
As I said, there are many different ways

40:24.880 --> 40:26.600
you can pull over the way.

40:27.760 --> 40:31.080
As already mentioned, but let me re-mention it,

40:31.080 --> 40:33.580
is to initialize the algorithm,

40:33.580 --> 40:36.460
we set the levels of all nodes to infinity

40:36.460 --> 40:40.100
except for the start vertex or origin vertex,

40:40.100 --> 40:41.900
whereby the level is set to zero

40:41.900 --> 40:45.660
and that vertex is placed into the open list

40:45.660 --> 40:46.740
or frontier list.

40:52.140 --> 40:54.420
Typically, in this setting, it's just a path length,

40:54.420 --> 40:55.900
so it would be just Euclidean distance

40:55.900 --> 40:57.020
between the two points.

40:58.700 --> 41:01.940
Or, in some cases, could be the Manhattan distance,

41:01.940 --> 41:03.460
so basically the path distance

41:03.460 --> 41:05.580
as you go through the edges of the grid.

41:17.300 --> 41:20.260
Yeah, so if you have that knowledge,

41:20.260 --> 41:23.060
it can be included into your discretization

41:23.060 --> 41:28.060
and that leads to multi-resolution discretization schemes

41:28.180 --> 41:33.180
that I mentioned before, whereby you discretize more finally

41:34.220 --> 41:35.980
in regions around the obstacles

41:35.980 --> 41:38.300
and you discretize less finally in open space.

41:38.300 --> 41:39.340
Yeah, absolutely.

41:39.340 --> 41:42.300
This is really the most basic version of the algorithm.

41:44.820 --> 41:47.220
So different ways of implementing the algorithm.

41:47.220 --> 41:52.220
How do we pull the nodes from the frontier list?

41:52.660 --> 41:54.780
One way, obviously, is to do it randomly,

41:54.780 --> 41:55.980
but there are better ways.

41:55.980 --> 41:58.700
So one way is to implement the strategy

41:58.700 --> 42:00.620
of last in, first out,

42:02.020 --> 42:03.820
which means that the last node

42:03.820 --> 42:06.060
that we have put in the frontier list

42:06.060 --> 42:08.420
is the first one that is going to be retrieved

42:08.420 --> 42:10.500
from the frontier list.

42:10.500 --> 42:14.620
This corresponds to a depth-first search strategy,

42:14.620 --> 42:18.500
whereby if we have a graph that looks like a tree,

42:18.500 --> 42:21.100
we first go deep into a branch

42:21.100 --> 42:23.660
before moving into the other branches.

42:26.980 --> 42:30.220
This is opposite to what bad-first search does.

42:30.220 --> 42:33.580
The idea is to keep Q as a list,

42:33.580 --> 42:37.500
whereby we apply the strategy first in, first out,

42:39.900 --> 42:41.900
which means that basically if we have a graph

42:41.900 --> 42:44.060
that looks like here as a tree,

42:46.940 --> 42:51.140
we first explore all the vertices at one level

42:51.140 --> 42:52.700
before going to the next level,

42:52.700 --> 42:57.700
because the first one that is going to be expanded

43:00.540 --> 43:05.540
is the first one that was put into the frontier list.

43:06.380 --> 43:08.540
So these two strategies are really orthogonal

43:08.540 --> 43:09.380
to each other.

43:11.740 --> 43:13.500
By the way, the bad-first search applied

43:13.500 --> 43:15.580
to level-correcting methods is also referred to

43:15.580 --> 43:17.940
as the Bellman-Ford algorithm for graph search.

43:18.060 --> 43:23.060
The last one, very popular,

43:23.060 --> 43:27.220
is to pick the node in the frontier Q

43:27.220 --> 43:29.820
that has the lowest cost to arrive,

43:29.820 --> 43:32.220
which arguably is the most natural.

43:32.220 --> 43:35.180
Since we're trying to find an optimal path,

43:35.180 --> 43:40.180
it's natural to consider as the candidate

43:40.460 --> 43:43.460
as the most promising node along the optimal path,

43:43.460 --> 43:45.540
the one that has the lowest cost to arrive.

43:46.540 --> 43:49.060
The downside of this approach, though,

43:49.060 --> 43:53.140
is that performing this operation can be expensive.

43:54.020 --> 43:56.260
So there are specialized data structures

43:56.260 --> 43:59.780
that allow you to perform this operation fairly efficiently,

43:59.780 --> 44:03.380
but in general, it takes a little bit longer.

44:03.380 --> 44:08.300
So for problems where you have several nodes,

44:08.300 --> 44:13.300
this could, so this operation could be time-consuming,

44:14.140 --> 44:16.620
but the advantage is that actually,

44:16.620 --> 44:18.980
the JAXA performs a number of steps

44:18.980 --> 44:21.580
that is lower in general than the other algorithms.

44:22.740 --> 44:24.540
And one of the key properties of the JAXA

44:24.540 --> 44:28.500
that is intuitive but not straightforward,

44:28.500 --> 44:30.620
you will need a proof that we're not going to do,

44:30.620 --> 44:33.220
is that under this exploration scheme,

44:33.220 --> 44:36.420
each node enters the frontier Q only once.

44:36.420 --> 44:38.860
In general, it might enter multiple times.

44:38.860 --> 44:41.740
We are guaranteed by the theorem that I stated before

44:41.740 --> 44:44.220
that these algorithms eventually will converge,

44:44.220 --> 44:46.300
but a node in general might enter

44:46.300 --> 44:47.780
the frontier Q many times.

44:47.780 --> 44:51.340
With the JAXA algorithm, it enters only once.

44:52.860 --> 44:54.340
Again, this would require proof.

44:54.340 --> 44:55.980
It's not entirely straightforward.

44:58.540 --> 45:00.100
Any question about a different,

45:01.180 --> 45:05.180
for your P-set, you're going to implement

45:05.180 --> 45:07.700
a variant of the JAXA algorithm.

45:07.700 --> 45:09.740
What are used in robotics?

45:10.420 --> 45:12.460
What do you mean about depth-first search?

45:12.460 --> 45:15.300
Usually it's not finding optimal path, right?

45:15.300 --> 45:18.780
Or unless you're exhausted researching the entire problem.

45:20.460 --> 45:23.220
When depth-first is executed

45:23.220 --> 45:25.420
within the level-correcting framework,

45:25.420 --> 45:27.700
it indeed finds the optimal path.

45:27.700 --> 45:29.900
But yes, it might eventually have to search

45:29.900 --> 45:32.380
the entire space absolutely.

45:32.380 --> 45:34.980
Otherwise, yes, you wouldn't find the optimal path.

45:34.980 --> 45:37.660
One advantage of the depth-first search

45:37.660 --> 45:40.500
is that you, at any point in time,

45:40.500 --> 45:42.940
you only need to store in memory a part of the graph

45:42.940 --> 45:44.900
because you go deep into a branch.

45:44.900 --> 45:47.100
Well, with depth-first, you may need to store

45:47.100 --> 45:49.020
the entire graph as you keep exploring.

45:51.620 --> 45:54.180
Okay, so this is again how,

45:54.180 --> 45:55.740
in particular, this is the JAXA algorithm,

45:55.740 --> 45:57.580
how it looks like.

45:57.580 --> 46:01.380
So you expand almost uniformly as a wavefront

46:01.380 --> 46:02.960
on the way to the goal.

46:02.960 --> 46:07.720
This is fine, but for example,

46:07.720 --> 46:12.080
assume that you had actually an environment

46:12.080 --> 46:14.240
that looked more like a circle.

46:14.240 --> 46:17.200
The expansion will be isotropic,

46:17.200 --> 46:19.080
which means that you will expand in this direction

46:19.080 --> 46:21.920
toward the goal, but also in the opposite direction.

46:21.920 --> 46:25.940
The reason is that in the level-correcting algorithm,

46:25.940 --> 46:30.240
even with the JAXA strategy for node selection,

46:30.240 --> 46:33.200
you only look at the cost to arrive,

46:33.200 --> 46:35.760
but you don't have any biasing term

46:35.760 --> 46:40.280
that tries to favor nodes that are closer to the goal.

46:40.280 --> 46:42.560
So that's why you have a wavefront

46:42.560 --> 46:44.040
that expands isotropically.

46:44.040 --> 46:47.560
You're only capturing the notion of cost to arrive,

46:47.560 --> 46:50.540
which does not capture any notion of where the goal is,

46:50.540 --> 46:52.720
where it's with your start region.

46:52.720 --> 46:57.720
So the point is that by running the JAXA,

46:58.000 --> 47:02.420
the JAXA, also referred to as uninformed search,

47:02.420 --> 47:03.920
because you don't use the information

47:03.920 --> 47:06.060
of where the goal is with respect to the start,

47:06.060 --> 47:07.800
you may be wasting some computation

47:07.800 --> 47:09.800
because you are searching in a region

47:09.800 --> 47:13.620
where probably you shouldn't search,

47:13.620 --> 47:18.160
which motivates the topic of informed search,

47:18.160 --> 47:22.020
how to bias the search toward those nodes

47:22.020 --> 47:25.840
that have more potential to lead you to the goal

47:25.840 --> 47:27.000
in a minimum cost.

47:27.720 --> 47:32.720
So as I said, by only account for uninformed,

47:35.840 --> 47:40.840
by only account for cost to arrive,

47:41.960 --> 47:44.200
we said that the search is uninformed.

47:44.200 --> 47:46.560
Now we're trying to include some information

47:46.560 --> 47:51.120
about where the goal is with respect to the start vertex

47:51.120 --> 47:54.760
in order to inform the search, bias it more toward the goal

47:54.760 --> 47:56.820
so that you don't waste effort in regions

47:56.820 --> 48:00.100
where you know that basically an optimal path

48:00.100 --> 48:01.760
is not going to go through.

48:03.460 --> 48:08.340
The idea, one of the key ideas is to order the nodes

48:08.340 --> 48:11.940
not only by cost to arrive, that is what the JAXA does,

48:11.940 --> 48:16.460
but by cost to arrive plus an approximation

48:16.460 --> 48:17.880
of the cost to go.

48:19.660 --> 48:23.100
In particular, that approximation has to be a lower bound

48:23.660 --> 48:28.660
for the cost of the path between the node Q and the goal.

48:31.260 --> 48:32.420
More specifically,

48:40.380 --> 48:45.380
let this be the goal, this is your start node,

48:46.260 --> 48:51.260
this is Q, you have found a path to Q,

48:53.260 --> 48:58.260
this is CQ, in standard JAXA nodes are ordered by CQ,

48:59.940 --> 49:01.740
and you pick from the frontier Q

49:01.740 --> 49:04.140
the node that has lowest CQ.

49:04.140 --> 49:09.140
The idea is to look at CQ plus an approximation

49:09.580 --> 49:11.060
of the cost to go.

49:13.320 --> 49:17.740
The cost to go is the cost of the path

49:17.740 --> 49:21.280
from node Q to the goal.

49:21.280 --> 49:23.120
You don't know the cost exactly

49:23.120 --> 49:26.800
because you haven't computed any paths yet.

49:26.800 --> 49:29.760
But in many cases, you have an approximation

49:29.760 --> 49:31.320
of that cost to go.

49:31.320 --> 49:33.900
For example, a very simple approximation

49:33.900 --> 49:38.400
is just a straight line that neglects all obstacles,

49:38.400 --> 49:40.240
so basically just Euclidean distance.

49:47.500 --> 49:48.920
Euclidean distance.

49:51.920 --> 49:56.920
So the idea then is to order the nodes

49:58.800 --> 50:03.420
by cost to arrive plus approximation of the cost to go.

50:03.420 --> 50:07.920
In particular, for this procedure to be well posed,

50:07.920 --> 50:11.600
the approximation of the cost to go, referred to as HQ,

50:12.760 --> 50:17.600
has to be a lower bound of the actual cost to go.

50:17.600 --> 50:22.600
Otherwise, you might not include nodes in your search

50:24.800 --> 50:27.880
that actually could be part of the optimal path.

50:27.880 --> 50:30.400
As I said, from all practical purposes,

50:30.400 --> 50:33.400
very common choice is just the Euclidean length

50:33.400 --> 50:36.560
of a straight line neglecting all obstacles.

50:36.560 --> 50:38.520
What really matters is that in this approximation,

50:38.520 --> 50:40.720
you have a lower bound, so this is lower

50:40.720 --> 50:44.600
than the actual optimal path from node Q to the goal.

50:44.600 --> 50:48.000
Second of all, it has to be something very quick to compute.

50:48.000 --> 50:50.580
Usually, straight line works very well.

50:50.580 --> 50:52.440
So then what you do is to order the nodes

50:52.440 --> 50:55.260
by cost arrival plus approximation of cost to go,

50:55.260 --> 50:59.600
so the function HQ, and if you're doing the test

50:59.600 --> 51:01.160
with respect to the upper bound,

51:01.160 --> 51:03.120
to speed up the algorithm even further,

51:03.120 --> 51:08.120
you strengthen the test by adding also the cost to go.

51:08.640 --> 51:13.520
The idea again is that if you have a node Q prime

51:14.200 --> 51:17.680
for which the cost to arrive plus the underestimate

51:17.680 --> 51:21.560
is larger than the best path that you have found so far

51:21.560 --> 51:24.560
to the goal, then it's worthless to consider it any longer.

51:26.480 --> 51:28.080
Key point is that in this way,

51:28.960 --> 51:32.760
fewer nodes are placed in the priority queue,

51:32.760 --> 51:34.280
and in particular, your P set,

51:34.280 --> 51:37.840
so this strategy is referred to as the A star algorithm,

51:37.840 --> 51:40.400
one of the main algorithms for graph search,

51:40.400 --> 51:42.640
and in your P set, you will implement it,

51:42.640 --> 51:45.840
you will be implementing exactly A star,

51:45.840 --> 51:48.160
and A star will be your reference planner

51:48.160 --> 51:49.820
for the rest of the quarter.

51:52.700 --> 51:54.760
So this is again the jextra.

51:54.760 --> 51:58.440
As you see, uniform search, you expand as a boyfriend.

51:58.440 --> 52:01.040
This will be the implementation with A star.

52:01.040 --> 52:05.400
You see how now you are favoring those nodes

52:05.400 --> 52:08.820
that are closer to the goal,

52:08.820 --> 52:10.960
so it's much more direct way of searching.

52:11.960 --> 52:15.720
The reason being that we include this approximate notion

52:15.720 --> 52:17.640
of how far we are from the goal

52:17.640 --> 52:19.860
in order to bias our search,

52:19.860 --> 52:23.100
so we don't waste effort around here or around here,

52:23.100 --> 52:24.720
because we already know that an optimal path

52:24.720 --> 52:26.080
wouldn't go through there.

52:27.520 --> 52:28.360
Yeah.

52:28.360 --> 52:31.120
Is this also outside the most optimal path?

52:31.120 --> 52:33.920
Yeah, by the way, there is no notion of most optimal path,

52:33.920 --> 52:35.560
the optimal path, yeah.

52:36.240 --> 52:40.280
All right, so to sum up,

52:40.280 --> 52:42.240
the advantage of grid-based approaches

52:42.240 --> 52:44.520
is that they are simple to use,

52:44.520 --> 52:46.300
and in general, very fast.

52:46.300 --> 52:49.960
The disadvantage is that since you rely on discretization,

52:49.960 --> 52:51.280
as you pointed out,

52:51.280 --> 52:54.840
there is the issue of how to select that discretization,

52:54.840 --> 52:57.080
that oftentimes is not very clear,

52:57.080 --> 53:00.700
and moreover, since you are discretizing an environment,

53:00.700 --> 53:03.800
the number of vertices scale exponentially

53:03.800 --> 53:06.880
with the number of dimensions.

53:06.880 --> 53:08.840
If you have a three-dimension,

53:08.840 --> 53:11.520
then the number of vertices will scale

53:11.520 --> 53:13.760
with two to the power of the dimensions.

53:13.760 --> 53:16.560
So three is fine, four is fine, five becomes a problem.

53:18.240 --> 53:20.480
Having said that, very useful approach,

53:20.480 --> 53:24.860
so make sure that you familiarize yourself with a star.

53:24.860 --> 53:27.880
And the first problem in the pset will help you to do so.

53:29.920 --> 53:33.000
All right, so this is a grid-based approach.

53:33.040 --> 53:35.920
Another approach is the one where we try

53:35.920 --> 53:39.040
to solve the problem directly in the continuous space

53:39.040 --> 53:42.120
by using data structures that retain all the information

53:42.120 --> 53:44.480
from the continuous formulation on the problem.

53:44.480 --> 53:45.800
I'll be a little bit faster here

53:45.800 --> 53:48.940
because this class of algorithms is useful conceptually,

53:48.940 --> 53:51.480
but is much less useful from a practical standpoint.

53:53.380 --> 53:56.280
So as we already said, the problem of motion planning

53:56.280 --> 53:59.280
is to plan the motion for a robot from initial configuration

53:59.280 --> 54:02.240
to a final configuration in a continuous space.

54:03.920 --> 54:08.600
Let's look again at the setup that we looked before.

54:08.600 --> 54:11.160
So for example, in this case, the problem is to find

54:11.160 --> 54:13.360
a sequence of translations and notations

54:13.360 --> 54:15.340
that deliver us to the goal region.

54:16.600 --> 54:18.120
This actually

54:22.440 --> 54:25.720
allow us to reinterpret the motion of planning,

54:25.720 --> 54:28.500
the problem of motion planning in a more unified way

54:28.500 --> 54:29.560
of what we've done so far.

54:29.560 --> 54:31.600
And this goes also back to the previous question

54:31.600 --> 54:34.880
about how do we account for the dimension of the robot.

54:34.880 --> 54:37.120
The problem of motion planning is really to find

54:37.120 --> 54:39.040
a sequence, a trajectory basically

54:39.040 --> 54:41.300
for your configuration variables.

54:41.300 --> 54:43.760
Once you've done that identification,

54:43.760 --> 54:46.160
the problem of motion planning reduces to the problem

54:46.160 --> 54:49.240
of planning the motion of a point in configuration space.

54:50.160 --> 54:53.360
So in general, the motion planning problem is defined

54:53.360 --> 54:55.240
with respect to the configuration space,

54:55.240 --> 54:57.960
meaning the set of all the configurations

54:57.960 --> 54:59.600
that the robot can assume.

54:59.640 --> 55:03.200
To be specific, in the case of two-dimensional robots,

55:03.200 --> 55:07.000
like for example a triangle, its configuration will be

55:07.000 --> 55:10.500
its Cartesian position x, y and its heading.

55:11.800 --> 55:14.400
So the problem of motion planning is to find a sequence

55:14.400 --> 55:19.400
for the x, y and theta so that the real robot

55:19.720 --> 55:24.720
do not collide with obstacles and reaches the goal.

55:25.480 --> 55:28.460
Now in the case, so the configuration in this case

55:28.500 --> 55:30.380
is a subset of R3.

55:30.380 --> 55:32.940
In reality, there is a nuance due to the fact

55:32.940 --> 55:36.140
that the theta is an angle and theta plus two pi

55:36.140 --> 55:37.600
is the same.

55:37.600 --> 55:39.980
So in reality, the correct way of representing this

55:39.980 --> 55:42.260
mathematically is a subset of R2.

55:42.260 --> 55:45.940
This is the Cartesian space for the position

55:45.940 --> 55:50.360
times the space for the rotation, so basically a circle.

55:50.360 --> 55:55.360
Why you have to account for the fact that theta plus two pi

55:55.520 --> 55:56.680
is equal to theta?

56:01.440 --> 56:02.340
Let's assume that,

56:08.140 --> 56:11.880
let's assume that, so this is zero and this is two pi

56:11.880 --> 56:15.000
and you do not recognize that zero is equal to two pi.

56:15.000 --> 56:19.000
So you may think that going in this direction

56:19.000 --> 56:22.860
is not possible, so basically swinging around from two pi

56:22.860 --> 56:24.200
to zero is not possible.

56:24.200 --> 56:28.080
So let's assume that this is your origin

56:28.080 --> 56:29.380
and this is your goal.

56:30.320 --> 56:33.080
So if you don't identify two pi with zero,

56:33.080 --> 56:35.440
then the motion that you will plan is something like this

56:35.440 --> 56:36.520
which is very long.

56:36.520 --> 56:38.880
If indeed you identify two pi with zero,

56:38.880 --> 56:40.720
then the motion that you will plan is this one

56:40.720 --> 56:41.720
that is much shorter.

56:45.640 --> 56:49.400
But so anyway, the point here is that in general,

56:49.400 --> 56:52.400
the motion planning problem and its full generality

56:52.400 --> 56:55.560
can be considered as a problem of finding a path

56:55.560 --> 56:58.840
in the configuration space, whereby the robot

56:58.840 --> 57:02.400
has been shrunk to a point and accordingly,

57:02.400 --> 57:05.840
the obstacles have been enlarged in order to account

57:05.840 --> 57:10.000
for the fact that the robot has been shrunk to a point.

57:10.000 --> 57:13.040
Like for example here, if we have a triangular,

57:14.700 --> 57:17.560
yeah, so we have a triangular obstacle and a circular robot

57:18.480 --> 57:20.760
in the configuration space, so in the space

57:20.760 --> 57:25.760
of xy positions, the obstacle will be represented

57:25.820 --> 57:30.820
as this inflated set, with the idea that if the xy position,

57:31.560 --> 57:33.900
so the center of your robot is here,

57:33.900 --> 57:36.400
then the true robot will collide with the obstacle.

57:36.400 --> 57:38.920
If it is outside, then the robot does not collide

57:38.920 --> 57:40.300
with the obstacle.

57:40.300 --> 57:43.480
So this goes back to the discussion we had before

57:43.480 --> 57:46.560
about the need to inflate the obstacles

57:46.560 --> 57:48.840
and basically represent, or more technically,

57:48.840 --> 57:51.220
to represent the obstacles in the configuration space

57:51.220 --> 57:54.480
in order to do motion planning, whereby we interpret

57:54.480 --> 57:58.660
the robot as a point, in particular, its configuration.

58:00.120 --> 58:02.040
It's a little subtle point, but something important

58:02.040 --> 58:03.520
to keep in mind.

58:03.520 --> 58:07.880
Is there any non-circular robots that are efficient

58:07.880 --> 58:10.480
possible ways to account for the robot's geometry

58:10.480 --> 58:11.320
when inflating the obstacles?

58:11.320 --> 58:13.400
Right, and the answer is no.

58:13.400 --> 58:16.780
That's why motion planning is a hard problem.

58:16.780 --> 58:17.840
Very good point.

58:17.840 --> 58:21.440
In general, how do we represent the obstacles

58:21.440 --> 58:24.460
that are in the workspace, so in the three dimensions,

58:24.460 --> 58:26.400
into the configuration space,

58:26.400 --> 58:28.660
which might be much higher dimensional?

58:28.660 --> 58:31.180
For example, if you have a robot with a manipulator,

58:31.180 --> 58:33.780
the configuration space accounts for the joint angles

58:33.780 --> 58:37.640
of your arm, plus the Cartesian position of the base

58:37.640 --> 58:39.900
of your manipulator, plus the heading,

58:39.900 --> 58:42.200
so the space could be like 10 dimensional.

58:42.200 --> 58:45.520
So now you have an obstacle in the workspace,

58:45.520 --> 58:49.000
so in the real world, you have to inflate it

58:49.000 --> 58:53.080
in the configuration space that has a different number

58:53.080 --> 58:55.320
of dimensions, in particular, much larger.

58:55.320 --> 58:57.840
How you do that, extremely complicated.

58:57.840 --> 59:00.440
That's exactly the reason why these combinatorial algorithms

59:00.440 --> 59:02.120
do not work in practice.

59:02.120 --> 59:04.360
Because they require an explicit representation

59:04.360 --> 59:06.680
of the obstacles in the configuration space

59:06.680 --> 59:10.640
that oftentimes, I'll say, almost always is not available,

59:10.640 --> 59:12.840
which motivates the sampling-based algorithms

59:12.840 --> 59:14.800
that we're going to discuss on Thursday.

59:15.640 --> 59:17.600
But yes, that's the entire point of these slides,

59:17.600 --> 59:21.200
to show that conceptually, if you can represent obstacles

59:21.200 --> 59:23.960
from the real world into the configuration space,

59:23.960 --> 59:26.660
where the motion planning problem really lives,

59:26.660 --> 59:29.160
then you can apply the algorithm that I'm going to mention

59:29.160 --> 59:31.320
in a second, and everything is easy.

59:31.320 --> 59:34.000
But in reality, this translation is very difficult.

59:34.000 --> 59:36.000
That's what motivates the sampling-based algorithms

59:36.000 --> 59:39.140
that we're going to discuss on Thursday.

59:40.000 --> 59:42.640
So this point here is a bit subtle.

59:42.640 --> 59:45.280
I would say that a good fraction of roboticists

59:45.280 --> 59:46.600
missed this point.

59:46.600 --> 59:49.200
I routinely review papers where this point is missed,

59:49.200 --> 59:52.800
and the claims that people make are completely nonsensical.

59:54.760 --> 59:57.200
So as I said, the conceptually, the problem, again,

59:57.200 --> 01:00:01.160
for the 10th time, is to shrink the point,

01:00:01.160 --> 01:00:03.840
the robot to a point, its configuration,

01:00:03.840 --> 01:00:06.040
which might be a high-dimensional object.

01:00:06.040 --> 01:00:08.400
The heading, the position, possibly the joints

01:00:08.400 --> 01:00:11.840
for the manipulator on top of your robot, and so on.

01:00:11.840 --> 01:00:16.840
But accordingly, you have to represent the obstacles

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:20.080
from the real world, referred to as workspace,

01:00:20.080 --> 01:00:23.160
into the configuration space.

01:00:23.160 --> 01:00:26.160
This mapping, in general, is complicated.

01:00:26.160 --> 01:00:28.800
In this case, whereby the dimension of the configuration

01:00:28.800 --> 01:00:31.760
space and the dimension of the workspace are the same,

01:00:31.760 --> 01:00:33.760
is very easy to understand.

01:00:33.760 --> 01:00:35.960
But in general, it is very difficult

01:00:35.960 --> 01:00:37.240
to represent explicitly.

01:00:37.240 --> 01:00:38.080
Yes?

01:00:38.080 --> 01:00:41.440
And what is the robot, like, modern robot?

01:00:41.440 --> 01:00:45.600
The robot arm can change shape, so potentially,

01:00:45.600 --> 01:00:49.200
in a string, the obstacles differently based on how you're,

01:00:49.200 --> 01:00:51.280
how the pose of the robot is.

01:00:51.280 --> 01:00:53.800
It can change shape in a sense that it's not a rigid body,

01:00:53.800 --> 01:00:55.200
it's a soft robot?

01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:58.040
No, but like, for example, if the arm is extended this way,

01:00:58.040 --> 01:01:00.800
then it's gonna have a larger kind of radius,

01:01:00.800 --> 01:01:03.800
but if the arm kind of contracts back to the body,

01:01:03.800 --> 01:01:06.400
then it's gonna be, it's gonna be smaller.

01:01:06.400 --> 01:01:09.360
So how do you account for that when you are inflating

01:01:09.360 --> 01:01:11.400
the obstacles, do you think?

01:01:11.400 --> 01:01:15.240
Right, so in general, representing explicitly

01:01:15.240 --> 01:01:19.200
obstacles from the workspace into configuration space

01:01:19.200 --> 01:01:21.820
of robot manipulator with multiple joints

01:01:21.820 --> 01:01:23.320
is extremely difficult.

01:01:23.320 --> 01:01:26.440
That's why this is basically never done explicitly

01:01:26.440 --> 01:01:29.160
like here, but it's done implicitly

01:01:29.160 --> 01:01:30.920
through the notion of sampling-based algorithms

01:01:30.920 --> 01:01:32.520
that we're going to see next time.

01:01:32.520 --> 01:01:35.160
But again, very good point, that's why these algorithms,

01:01:35.160 --> 01:01:38.260
even though we're going to spend 50 minutes on them,

01:01:38.260 --> 01:01:39.980
more for self consistency of this class,

01:01:39.980 --> 01:01:42.800
are not really that useful in practice,

01:01:42.800 --> 01:01:44.760
because of, for example, this problem.

01:01:46.560 --> 01:01:51.560
Okay, having said that, in the context of planning

01:01:51.560 --> 01:01:55.260
configuration space, then the problem formally becomes

01:01:55.260 --> 01:01:58.280
the problem finding a continuous path

01:01:58.280 --> 01:02:03.280
for your configuration vector that maps the interval zero,

01:02:04.160 --> 01:02:08.920
one into a trajectory that does not intersect

01:02:08.920 --> 01:02:11.960
with any obstacle, so it belongs to the free portion

01:02:11.960 --> 01:02:15.440
of the configuration space, with a property that at zero,

01:02:15.440 --> 01:02:17.040
we are at the initial node, at the end,

01:02:17.040 --> 01:02:18.660
we are at the goal node.

01:02:18.660 --> 01:02:20.600
The free portion of the configuration space

01:02:20.600 --> 01:02:25.600
is the set of configuration such that when the robot

01:02:25.720 --> 01:02:28.820
in the workspace is at that configuration,

01:02:28.820 --> 01:02:31.200
it does not intersect with an obstacle.

01:02:33.760 --> 01:02:34.800
Does that make sense?

01:02:36.540 --> 01:02:39.000
So that's really the link between configuration space

01:02:39.000 --> 01:02:40.040
and workspace.

01:02:40.040 --> 01:02:43.300
The collision checking is done obviously in the workspace,

01:02:43.300 --> 01:02:46.800
and this is the formula that allows you to translate

01:02:46.800 --> 01:02:50.400
conceptually obstacles in the configuration space

01:02:50.400 --> 01:02:53.700
into constraints, sorry, obstacles in the workspace

01:02:53.700 --> 01:02:56.280
into constraints in the configuration space.

01:02:56.280 --> 01:02:58.200
Unfortunately, there is no general algorithm

01:02:58.200 --> 01:03:01.240
that will allow you to do that explicitly and efficiently.

01:03:01.240 --> 01:03:03.080
Does that mean that the free space is different

01:03:03.080 --> 01:03:05.520
for different orientations of the robot?

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:07.760
Or for different configurations of the robot?

01:03:07.760 --> 01:03:10.820
So the free space is the set of all configurations

01:03:10.820 --> 01:03:14.080
such that the robot does not intersect an obstacle

01:03:14.080 --> 01:03:15.240
in the workspace.

01:03:15.240 --> 01:03:17.400
So the configuration space is a space,

01:03:17.400 --> 01:03:20.960
so it's a set of an uncountable number of configurations.

01:03:20.960 --> 01:03:23.120
All right, so having said that,

01:03:23.120 --> 01:03:27.200
the idea of combinatorial planning is to steal

01:03:27.720 --> 01:03:31.360
a computer roadmap, so basically a graph,

01:03:31.360 --> 01:03:33.160
similar to grid-based search,

01:03:33.160 --> 01:03:37.120
but in a way that we are not losing any information

01:03:37.120 --> 01:03:40.460
with respect to the underlying continuous problem.

01:03:41.480 --> 01:03:44.000
This means that this roadmap needs to have

01:03:44.000 --> 01:03:45.640
two key properties.

01:03:45.640 --> 01:03:50.640
Accessibility, which means that for every configuration,

01:03:52.560 --> 01:03:54.480
QI, so initial configuration,

01:03:54.480 --> 01:03:56.740
and the final configuration of Q goal,

01:03:57.300 --> 01:03:59.900
there is a way to connect this configuration

01:03:59.900 --> 01:04:02.960
to at least a node in the roadmap.

01:04:02.960 --> 01:04:07.400
So you don't end up in situations whereby, say,

01:04:07.400 --> 01:04:08.400
where is it?

01:04:23.760 --> 01:04:27.480
So there's an obstacle, this is your QI,

01:04:27.480 --> 01:04:32.240
and this is your roadmap, so basically this is the graph

01:04:32.240 --> 01:04:35.240
that you use to represent your continuous space.

01:04:35.240 --> 01:04:37.840
So this is a case whereby you would like to solve

01:04:37.840 --> 01:04:39.920
the motion planning problem from here,

01:04:39.920 --> 01:04:44.000
but you don't have an easy way to connect QI

01:04:44.000 --> 01:04:47.440
to any node in the roadmap, so there will be a bad roadmap.

01:04:48.360 --> 01:04:52.680
And connectivity, if there exists a path from QI to QG,

01:04:52.680 --> 01:04:56.080
there exists a path on the roadmap from S1 to S2.

01:04:56.080 --> 01:04:59.320
This basically means that you're not losing any information

01:04:59.320 --> 01:05:01.080
by having discrete representation

01:05:01.080 --> 01:05:02.840
of your underlying continuous space.

01:05:05.920 --> 01:05:10.100
Because given QI and QG, I can always connect those

01:05:10.100 --> 01:05:12.400
to at least a node in the roadmap,

01:05:12.400 --> 01:05:14.140
and there is always a path in the roadmap

01:05:14.140 --> 01:05:19.140
that brings me to the node S2 that I can connect to QG.

01:05:22.000 --> 01:05:23.080
Does that make sense?

01:05:24.720 --> 01:05:26.960
So the question, of course, is how do you compute

01:05:26.960 --> 01:05:29.600
a roadmap with these two properties?

01:05:29.600 --> 01:05:31.320
If you have these two properties,

01:05:31.320 --> 01:05:34.480
then you're guaranteed that the roadmap provides

01:05:34.480 --> 01:05:36.640
discrete representation of the continuous motion planning

01:05:36.640 --> 01:05:39.000
problem without losing any of the original

01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:41.800
connectivity information needed to solve it.

01:05:41.800 --> 01:05:44.080
The question now, of course, is how do you compute

01:05:44.080 --> 01:05:44.920
such a roadmap?

01:05:47.260 --> 01:05:50.640
So in the case of 2D, and again, the reason why I use

01:05:50.640 --> 01:05:53.700
the 2D case is A, because it's easy to visualize,

01:05:53.700 --> 01:05:56.340
two, because this class of algorithm work well

01:05:56.340 --> 01:05:59.160
for the simple problems that don't really work well

01:05:59.160 --> 01:06:00.880
for more complicated problems.

01:06:00.880 --> 01:06:03.480
A simple way to compute such a roadmap

01:06:03.480 --> 01:06:07.280
is to use the notion of self-decomposition,

01:06:07.280 --> 01:06:10.600
whereby the environment is decomposing to cells

01:06:10.600 --> 01:06:15.600
that are easy to compute, and then connectivity

01:06:15.800 --> 01:06:18.440
within each cell is straightforward.

01:06:18.440 --> 01:06:20.820
For example, you consider cells that are convex,

01:06:20.820 --> 01:06:22.820
which means that between any two nodes,

01:06:22.820 --> 01:06:24.680
you have an interpolating line that belongs

01:06:24.680 --> 01:06:25.740
to that set as well.

01:06:27.080 --> 01:06:30.880
And finally, the adiancy between the cells

01:06:30.880 --> 01:06:32.920
is also straightforward.

01:06:33.200 --> 01:06:36.040
So a simple way to compute a cell decomposition

01:06:36.040 --> 01:06:39.960
is by computing a trapezoidal cell decomposition.

01:06:39.960 --> 01:06:42.640
Trapezoidal cell decomposition works as follows.

01:06:42.640 --> 01:06:47.640
You start from each vertex of the forbidden space

01:06:48.320 --> 01:06:52.340
of obstacle space, like for example here or here,

01:06:52.340 --> 01:06:56.220
and you start shooting rays downward and upward

01:06:56.220 --> 01:07:00.460
until you intersect the boundary of the feasible space.

01:07:01.460 --> 01:07:03.300
Okay?

01:07:03.300 --> 01:07:07.980
Every time you have an intersection, you build a cell.

01:07:07.980 --> 01:07:11.220
And then you place a representative point in,

01:07:11.220 --> 01:07:14.780
for example, at the center, at the centroid of that cell.

01:07:14.780 --> 01:07:16.940
In this way, you're guaranteed that each cell

01:07:16.940 --> 01:07:21.500
is indeed convex, so you have the first accessibility

01:07:21.500 --> 01:07:24.740
property, and also due to the adiancy with which

01:07:24.740 --> 01:07:27.640
you compute these trapezoidal cell decomposition,

01:07:27.640 --> 01:07:29.580
you also have the connectivity property.

01:07:30.640 --> 01:07:32.880
So then the way that motion planning would work

01:07:32.880 --> 01:07:35.720
is that if you're given a QI here,

01:07:35.720 --> 01:07:37.680
you connect it to the closest,

01:07:38.780 --> 01:07:42.540
well, you connect it to the centroid of the cell

01:07:42.540 --> 01:07:45.620
where QI lies, then you do planning by using,

01:07:45.620 --> 01:07:48.380
for example, A star on the roadmap,

01:07:48.380 --> 01:07:52.620
and then you connect the QG to the node

01:07:52.620 --> 01:07:54.720
that is representative in the same cell.

01:07:54.720 --> 01:07:59.160
This is a purely geometric formulation

01:07:59.160 --> 01:08:00.480
of motion planning problem.

01:08:04.000 --> 01:08:04.840
Yep?

01:08:04.840 --> 01:08:08.440
So the vertices are not necessarily all sharp points,

01:08:08.440 --> 01:08:11.680
but on the space, there's some curves,

01:08:11.680 --> 01:08:15.520
so you can't necessarily draw a streamline trapezoid.

01:08:15.520 --> 01:08:20.520
Yeah, so these algorithms are simple to explain

01:08:21.140 --> 01:08:24.160
and to visualize, to implement, are really a pain,

01:08:24.160 --> 01:08:29.040
because of basically doing these geometric constructions,

01:08:29.040 --> 01:08:30.940
like computation is not easy.

01:08:30.940 --> 01:08:34.260
There is a tool that I'm going to mention in a second,

01:08:34.260 --> 01:08:37.520
but in general, coding this stuff is not easy,

01:08:37.520 --> 01:08:39.360
and then if you start having curves,

01:08:39.360 --> 01:08:41.880
then you have to do some polygonal approximation

01:08:41.880 --> 01:08:43.440
in order to run the scheme.

01:08:44.720 --> 01:08:47.120
So these algorithms are easy to explain

01:08:47.120 --> 01:08:49.880
and easy to understand, but they're difficult

01:08:49.880 --> 01:08:52.760
to implement and not very practical.

01:08:52.760 --> 01:08:55.720
So again, this is just, I'm talking about these algorithms

01:08:55.720 --> 01:08:59.400
more for your knowledge, but what you really need to learn

01:08:59.400 --> 01:09:02.200
from this class is the A star search algorithm,

01:09:02.200 --> 01:09:04.940
from this lecture, the A star search algorithm.

01:09:04.940 --> 01:09:07.480
There are other ways to build the roadmap,

01:09:07.480 --> 01:09:10.120
so like for example, visibility graphs

01:09:10.120 --> 01:09:13.520
that allow you to reason about optimality,

01:09:13.520 --> 01:09:16.360
basically beyond the scope of this class.

01:09:18.120 --> 01:09:20.240
As we already mentioned, a key difficulty

01:09:20.280 --> 01:09:22.800
with these algorithms is that we are assuming

01:09:22.800 --> 01:09:25.760
that we can represent in the computational space

01:09:25.760 --> 01:09:28.440
the set of configurations that are obstacle-free.

01:09:28.440 --> 01:09:31.680
In general, this is very hard to do.

01:09:31.680 --> 01:09:33.160
Going back to your question,

01:09:36.160 --> 01:09:39.880
there are actually computational procedures to do it,

01:09:39.880 --> 01:09:41.880
but they have a terrible scaling,

01:09:41.880 --> 01:09:44.280
like double exponential and these kind of things.

01:09:44.280 --> 01:09:47.280
So conceptually, it's possible,

01:09:47.280 --> 01:09:48.960
practically, it's not possible.

01:09:50.320 --> 01:09:52.800
And by stretching my drawing skills,

01:09:52.800 --> 01:09:57.040
I want to give you yet another explanation

01:09:57.040 --> 01:10:02.040
about why it's so hard to go from the world space

01:10:02.960 --> 01:10:06.840
to the representation of the obstacles in the world space

01:10:06.840 --> 01:10:08.400
into a representation of the obstacles

01:10:08.400 --> 01:10:09.860
in the configuration space.

01:10:09.860 --> 01:10:11.280
Let's go back to the previous example,

01:10:11.280 --> 01:10:13.280
so moving in triangles from initial position

01:10:13.280 --> 01:10:14.360
to the goal position.

01:10:15.200 --> 01:10:16.800
The configuration space is three-dimensional.

01:10:16.800 --> 01:10:20.180
You have translations, X, Y, and the theta, the heading.

01:10:22.720 --> 01:10:26.480
So for example, we can take any point on the triangle

01:10:26.480 --> 01:10:28.280
to be our representative point,

01:10:28.280 --> 01:10:29.780
like for example, this vertex.

01:10:31.120 --> 01:10:34.600
So the first thing to do is to, as we did say before,

01:10:34.600 --> 01:10:37.980
to enlarge the obstacles by using the geometry

01:10:37.980 --> 01:10:38.820
of the robots.

01:10:39.800 --> 01:10:42.680
So if your vertex, upper left vertex is here,

01:10:42.680 --> 01:10:45.240
then, so okay, so here I'm only considering translation,

01:10:45.240 --> 01:10:46.760
sorry, so in this problem,

01:10:46.760 --> 01:10:48.520
the configuration is two-dimensional.

01:10:49.400 --> 01:10:52.520
So if my upper vertex is here,

01:10:52.520 --> 01:10:55.520
two robots will be in collision, otherwise it is not.

01:10:55.520 --> 01:10:58.600
So it slides this robot along the shape of the obstacle,

01:10:58.600 --> 01:11:01.640
and this will be the enlarged version of the obstacle

01:11:01.640 --> 01:11:04.660
in the configuration space that will lead it to a collision

01:11:04.660 --> 01:11:07.360
for the two robots in the world space.

01:11:07.360 --> 01:11:08.920
Same for the other obstacle.

01:11:09.760 --> 01:11:12.760
Then I have to trim the environment,

01:11:12.760 --> 01:11:16.480
and having done that, then in the configuration space,

01:11:16.480 --> 01:11:18.480
which is just X, Y for the triangular robot,

01:11:18.480 --> 01:11:20.540
we are not allowing for now rotation,

01:11:20.540 --> 01:11:23.940
the problem is just to find a path for a point.

01:11:23.940 --> 01:11:25.480
By using, for example, either a star,

01:11:25.480 --> 01:11:28.400
or the trapezoidal side of the composition, and so on.

01:11:28.400 --> 01:11:30.600
But let's now put the heading

01:11:30.600 --> 01:11:31.700
as an additional degree of freedom.

01:11:31.700 --> 01:11:33.800
So now the robot can also rotate.

01:11:33.800 --> 01:11:36.960
So now the world space is two-dimensional,

01:11:36.960 --> 01:11:39.240
but the configuration space is three-dimensional.

01:11:39.240 --> 01:11:40.600
You have X, Y, and theta,

01:11:40.600 --> 01:11:43.600
which means that you have to do that sliding procedure

01:11:43.600 --> 01:11:48.440
to increase the obstacles for each fixed value of theta,

01:11:48.440 --> 01:11:51.200
which will give rise to this very weird shape

01:11:51.200 --> 01:11:52.620
in three dimensions.

01:11:52.620 --> 01:11:56.960
So those two seemingly benign obstacles in 2D

01:11:56.960 --> 01:11:58.720
becomes these two horrible things

01:11:58.720 --> 01:12:00.160
in the configuration space.

01:12:01.320 --> 01:12:04.240
And this is just to go from a two-dimensional world space

01:12:04.240 --> 01:12:06.920
into a three-dimensional configuration space.

01:12:06.920 --> 01:12:10.340
Think about six-dimensional configuration spaces

01:12:10.940 --> 01:12:12.020
with dynamics.

01:12:12.020 --> 01:12:13.620
No way you can do it.

01:12:13.620 --> 01:12:17.120
Which motivates the algorithm I'm going to discuss

01:12:17.120 --> 01:12:20.140
on Thursday, which is the class of sampling-based algorithms.

01:12:20.140 --> 01:12:23.660
Before leaving, though, so there was a question about

01:12:23.660 --> 01:12:25.700
if you really want to apply combinatorial algorithms,

01:12:25.700 --> 01:12:26.540
how you do it.

01:12:27.580 --> 01:12:30.460
So dealing with the geometric construction

01:12:30.460 --> 01:12:32.840
is actually not easy.

01:12:32.840 --> 01:12:35.180
Part of my PhD was in doing so,

01:12:35.180 --> 01:12:36.900
and it was really pain in the neck.

01:12:36.900 --> 01:12:39.820
Fortunately, there is a library called SIGAL,

01:12:40.180 --> 01:12:42.020
the computational geometry algorithm library

01:12:42.020 --> 01:12:45.740
that I extensively leveraged for my PhD

01:12:45.740 --> 01:12:49.100
that allows you to do a number of primitive

01:12:49.100 --> 01:12:52.160
geometric computation, like computing Boronoi diagrams

01:12:52.160 --> 01:12:54.200
and a lot of things.

01:12:54.200 --> 01:12:56.380
Taffy-Seidel set the composition and so on

01:12:56.380 --> 01:12:58.260
in a very simple way.

01:12:58.260 --> 01:13:01.520
So if you want to use combinatorial algorithms

01:13:01.520 --> 01:13:04.740
and in particular geometry computation,

01:13:04.740 --> 01:13:07.660
I strongly encourage you to look at the SIGAL.

01:13:08.580 --> 01:13:11.340
This link, you can find, you should click on it.

01:13:11.340 --> 01:13:13.340
You can find an interesting visualization

01:13:13.340 --> 01:13:15.860
of the computational space for a polygonal robot.

01:13:15.860 --> 01:13:18.100
This gives you yet more insight

01:13:18.100 --> 01:13:19.400
on the challenge of motion planning,

01:13:19.400 --> 01:13:21.420
which is again this translation from the workspace

01:13:21.420 --> 01:13:23.380
to the computational space.

01:13:23.380 --> 01:13:26.780
And finally, some additional readings if you're interested.

01:13:26.780 --> 01:13:29.860
So to sum up, for combinatorial planning algorithms,

01:13:29.860 --> 01:13:32.460
the advantage is that these approaches are complete.

01:13:32.460 --> 01:13:35.900
If there is a feasible path, they will find it,

01:13:35.900 --> 01:13:38.900
and they also have better guarantees on the running time.

01:13:38.900 --> 01:13:42.000
Unfortunately, due to this complication

01:13:42.000 --> 01:13:45.860
of translating obstacles from the workspace

01:13:45.860 --> 01:13:48.220
to the computational space in explicit manner,

01:13:48.220 --> 01:13:50.660
as required by combinatorial algorithms,

01:13:50.660 --> 01:13:53.860
running these algorithms on problems

01:13:53.860 --> 01:13:57.140
with more than three or four degrees of freedom

01:13:57.140 --> 01:13:59.540
is not really practical.

01:13:59.540 --> 01:14:03.260
And also, they're difficult to implement.

01:14:03.260 --> 01:14:05.420
I gave you a reference for a library

01:14:05.780 --> 01:14:07.060
that would ease the implementation,

01:14:07.060 --> 01:14:08.980
but they're still very tricky to implement.

01:14:08.980 --> 01:14:12.300
The algorithms that I'm going to discuss on Thursday

01:14:12.300 --> 01:14:14.820
actually are much simpler to implement

01:14:14.820 --> 01:14:16.300
and scale much better with respect

01:14:16.300 --> 01:14:17.740
to the number of dimensions.

01:14:18.620 --> 01:14:22.020
And in particular, we look at those algorithms on Thursday.

01:14:22.020 --> 01:14:23.540
So to sum up this lecture,

01:14:23.540 --> 01:14:26.580
I talked about grid-based approaches to motion planning.

01:14:26.580 --> 01:14:29.660
This is really what you have to remember from this lecture.

01:14:29.660 --> 01:14:31.540
It's going to, it's a very important tool

01:14:31.540 --> 01:14:33.540
in the field of robotics, and you're going to use

01:14:33.540 --> 01:14:36.540
in particular A star for the rest of the quarter.

01:14:36.540 --> 01:14:39.060
As a bonus material, I talked a little bit

01:14:39.060 --> 01:14:41.860
about combinatorial planning,

01:14:41.860 --> 01:14:44.100
with the idea that conceptually this is

01:14:44.100 --> 01:14:46.180
a very interesting approach to motion planning,

01:14:46.180 --> 01:14:48.100
practically not that useful.

01:14:48.100 --> 01:14:51.460
For your midterm, I will ask you conceptual questions

01:14:51.460 --> 01:14:54.840
about grid-based approaches, like for example,

01:14:54.840 --> 01:14:56.620
what's the difference between Dijkstra

01:14:56.620 --> 01:14:58.100
and the breadth-first search,

01:14:58.100 --> 01:15:01.360
and questions about sampling-based algorithms

01:15:01.360 --> 01:15:02.780
that I will explain on Thursday.

01:15:02.860 --> 01:15:05.060
I won't ask questions about combinatorial planning

01:15:05.060 --> 01:15:08.700
since, as I said, it was more like a bonus introduction

01:15:08.700 --> 01:15:12.500
to this material, but I really didn't go into the details.

01:15:12.500 --> 01:15:13.580
Okay?

01:15:13.580 --> 01:15:15.560
So I'll see you on Thursday.

